{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "prop:honest-outer-ci",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "As an immediate corollary of the simultaneous-event theorem, \\(\\widehat{\\mathrm{CI}}_n=[\\widehat L_n,\\widehat U_n]\\) satisfies \\(\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\\inf_{P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}:\\,\\mathcal C_P\\neq\\varnothing}P\\{\\Theta(P)\\subseteq\\widehat{\\mathrm{CI}}_n\\}\\ge1-\\alpha\\). The interval is intersected with \\([-K,K]\\), and coverage allows \\(R(P)=0\\), nonunique optimizers, and binding obstacle constraints.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "P",
      "\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}",
      "\\mathcal C_P",
      "\\Theta(P)",
      "\\widehat{\\mathrm{CI}}_n",
      "\\alpha",
      "K",
      "R(P)",
      "\\widehat L_n",
      "\\widehat U_n"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:uniform-band-to-program",
      "thm:attained-interval",
      "def:boundary-band-handle"
    ],
    "justification": "Uniform coverage is the direct reportable consequence of the root inclusion-event and endpoint theorem.",
    "gap": "Kaido, Molinari, and Stoye (2019) treat finite moment systems, whereas this corollary inherits a primitive uniform boundary-regression and signed-measure inclusion theorem for a continuum completion class. It does not characterize the source paper's separate parametric estimator.",
    "consumer": "County-level temperature and other continuously changing policy panels can report one confidence interval covering every full-law-compatible WAOSS value.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) such that \\(\\mathcal C_P\\neq\\varnothing\\).  By \\(\\text{thm:uniform-band-to-program}\\), on the event \\(\\mathcal E_n(P)\\) the population tuple associated with every \\(u\\in\\mathcal C_P\\) is feasible for the empirical program, and its selected endpoints satisfy\n\\[\n \\widehat L_n\\leq \\frac{T-V_P^+}{A}\n \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n \\widehat U_n\\geq \\frac{T-V_P^-}{A}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe denominator in (1) is positive: membership of \\(P\\) in \\(\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) entails the denominator-separation condition \\(A\\geq a_0>0\\).  Since \\(V_P^-\\leq V_P^+\\), division by \\(A>0\\) gives\n\\[\n \\frac{T-V_P^+}{A}\\leq \\frac{T-V_P^-}{A}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nBy \\(\\text{thm:attained-interval}\\), nonemptiness of \\(\\mathcal C_P\\) implies\n\\[\n \\Theta(P)=\\left[\\frac{T-V_P^+}{A},\\frac{T-V_P^-}{A}\\right]\n \\subseteq[-K,K].\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nCombining (1)--(3) with the definition\n\\(\\widehat{\\mathrm{CI}}_n=[\\widehat L_n,\\widehat U_n]\\) yields the event inclusion\n\\[\n \\mathcal E_n(P)\n \\subseteq\n \\left\\{\\Theta(P)\\subseteq\\widehat{\\mathrm{CI}}_n\\right\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nIndeed, on \\(\\mathcal E_n(P)\\), (1)--(2) place the left endpoint of \\(\\Theta(P)\\) weakly above \\(\\widehat L_n\\) and its right endpoint weakly below \\(\\widehat U_n\\).  The same theorem also guarantees Borel measurability of the lexicographically selected endpoints, so the event on the right of (4) is measurable.\n\nTaking probabilities in (4) gives, for every admissible \\(P\\),\n\\[\n P\\!\\left\\{\\Theta(P)\\subseteq\\widehat{\\mathrm{CI}}_n\\right\\}\n \\geq P\\{\\mathcal E_n(P)\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nInfimizing (5) over\n\\(\\{P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}:\\mathcal C_P\\neq\\varnothing\\}\\), taking lower limits, and invoking the uniform probability assertion of \\(\\text{thm:uniform-band-to-program}\\) proves\n\\[\n \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\n \\inf_{P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}:\\,\\mathcal C_P\\neq\\varnothing}\n P\\!\\left\\{\\Theta(P)\\subseteq\\widehat{\\mathrm{CI}}_n\\right\\}\n \\geq\n \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\n \\inf_{P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}:\\,\\mathcal C_P\\neq\\varnothing}\n P\\{\\mathcal E_n(P)\\}\n \\geq 1-\\alpha.\n\\]\nFinally, (3) shows why intersecting the empirical interval with \\([-K,K]\\) cannot delete any element of the target set.  No step uses \\(R(P)>0\\), strict obstacle inequalities, or uniqueness of an optimizer.  Hence the argument also covers \\(R(P)=0\\), binding obstacle constraints, and nonunique optimizers.",
